---
title: Host linear algebra
summary: skoro-dense. f64 Cholesky, triangular and SPD solves, column-pivoted QR least squares, and the whitening of a precision on the host via faer, and why the estimator's solve lives outside the graph.
order: 10
---

`skoro-dense` is host-resident dense linear algebra in `f64`, one matrix at a time, backed by
[faer](https://faer.veganb.tw/). It depends only on `skoro-backend` and `skoro-core`, so nothing that
merely uses `Normal` pays for `faer`.

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-dense/src/lib.rs:1..6}
```

A `Dense` is always exactly one matrix. A batch of independent problems crosses the host boundary in
one transfer (`Dense::split_tensor` / `split_vector` / `stack_vector`) and is then solved row by row,
so the batch axis is handled *around* the factorizations rather than inside them.

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-dense/src/lib.rs:15..29}
```

## The six entry points

`skoro_dense::factor` has six functions, all in the `skoro` prelude and all returning
`SkoroResult`: `cholesky`, `solve_lower`, `solve_upper`, `spd_solve`, `lstsq`, and
`whitening_from_precision`. The last turns a precision $\Lambda$ into a lower-triangular $W$ with
$W\transpose W = \Lambda$, packed for a `Tril` quantity, which is the host step a marginalization
needs to attach its joint factor.

## The boundary is two tensors wide

In an estimator iteration, only the Jacobian $J$ (in) and the step $\delta$ (out) cross the
boundary. Everything between is host `f64` and has no representation in the graph.
`Dense::split_tensor` moves a whole batch across in one transfer and slices it on the host.

## Why QR on the host

The damped step solves

$$
(J\transpose J + \lambda I)\,\delta = -J\transpose r
\iff
\min_\delta \left\lVert \begin{bmatrix} J \\ \sqrt\lambda\, I \end{bmatrix}\delta - \begin{bmatrix} -r \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\rVert .
$$

Solving the right-hand form by QR keeps the conditioning at $\kappa$ instead of $\kappa^2$ at *every*
damping value, and burn has no QR. That settles where the solve runs.

> [!DECISION]
> See [Dense solves on the host](../../design/host-dense-linear-algebra/).

## Keeping two triangular solves apart

`TrilInvert` (in `skoro-linalg`) is a differentiable graph operation on every backend, and it stays
there. `skoro-dense`'s triangular solve is for estimators. A shared test checks that the two compute
the same inverse.
